 "vyzhimka": {
  "tekst": "I’d be cautious with Business Intelligence DNA (businessinteldna.com). I investigated the domain and its public footprint, and I could not establish who is actually behind it or verify it as an established company.\n\nThe strongest finding is that businessinteldna.com appears to be extremely new. A domain-registration index lists it among .com domains newly registered on September 6, 2026—only about 10 days ago as of September 16, 2026. ([com.all-url.info](https://com.all-url.info/21/25/?utm_source=openai)) The site is currently live and describes itself in Russian as “Business Intelligence DNA — an intelligence system for business and experts,” but the publicly indexed site provides very little identifying corporate information. ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/))\n\nI also searched specifically for the domain, the Business Intelligence DNA name, corporate registrations, LinkedIn presence, and people associated with it. I did not find an independently verifiable founder/owner, established company profile, meaningful operating history, or third-party coverage tied to this particular business. Search results for “Business Intelligence DNA” mostly refer to unrelated organizations or generic uses of that phrase. ([accantec.de](https://www.accantec.de/presse/accantec-group-startet-gestaerkt-in-das-geschaeftsjahr-2024?utm_source=openai))\n\nThat does not prove it is a scam. A legitimate new startup can have exactly this kind of footprint. But at present I would classify it as “unverified/new,” rather than a company whose legitimacy can be independently established.\n\nThe combination I would pay attention to is:\n\n- Domain apparently created September 6, 2026.\n- No clear, independently verifiable person or established business entity that I could connect to the website.\n- Virtually no independent web history for the exact domain/business.\n- The website's claims currently appear to be primarily self-published rather than corroborated by outside sources. ([com.all-url.info](https://com.all-url.info/21/25/?utm_source=openai))\n\nIf someone from Business Intelligence DNA has approached you about an investment, employment opportunity, business purchase, consulting engagement, or asked you to provide money/identity/banking information, I would not rely on the website itself as proof of legitimacy.\n\nIf you give me the name/email/phone number of the person who contacted you (or a screenshot of the message), I can investigate that person, domain-registration clues, corporate records, social profiles, and any connections to other businesses. That would probably tell us considerably more about who is actually operating it.",
